**Florida Statutes Chapter 30** (particularly **§ 30.15**) is the primary state statute defining the powers, duties, and obligations of county sheriffs. The office itself is established by the Florida Constitution (Art. VIII, § 1(d)), which requires an elected sheriff in each county. Statutes then detail the authority.

### Core Powers and Duties under § 30.15(1)
Sheriffs (in person or by deputy), in their respective counties, **shall**:

- **(a)** Execute all process of the Supreme Court, circuit courts, county courts, and boards of county commissioners of this state, to be executed in their counties.
- **(b)** Execute such other writs, processes, warrants, and other papers directed to them, as may come to their hands to be executed in their counties.
- **(c)** Attend all sessions of the circuit court and county court held in their counties.
- **(d)** Execute all orders of the boards of county commissioners of their counties (with compensation from the county treasury as the boards deem proper).
- **(e)** Be **conservators of the peace** in their counties.
- **(f)** Suppress tumults, riots, and unlawful assemblies in their counties with force and strong hand when necessary.
- **(g)** Apprehend, without warrant, any person disturbing the peace, and carry that person before the proper judicial officer for further proceedings according to law.
- **(h)** Have authority to raise the power of the county (posse comitatus) and command any person to assist them when necessary in the execution of their duties. Refusal (unless physically incompetent) is punishable by up to 1 year in jail or a fine up to $500.
- **(i)** Be, ex officio, timber agents for their counties.
- **(j)** Perform such other duties as may be imposed upon them by law.
- **(k)** Assist district school boards, charter school governing boards, private schools, and certain child care facilities regarding school safety options under § 1006.12. This includes (at minimum) providing access to the Chris Hixon, Coach Aaron Feis, and Coach Scott Beigel Guardian Program for preventing or abating active assailant incidents. The statute contains detailed requirements for training, certification, psychological evaluation, drug testing, ongoing qualifications, reporting to the Florida Department of Law Enforcement (FDLE), and related procedures for school guardians and school security guards (who generally lack full arrest powers except as needed for active assailant response).

### Exclusive Policing Jurisdiction (§ 30.15(5), added/clarified in recent legislation)
- There **shall** be an elected sheriff in each Florida county, and the transfer of the sheriff’s duties to another officer or office is prohibited.
- Unless otherwise authorized by state law, the sheriff has **exclusive policing jurisdiction in the unincorporated areas** of each county.
- The sheriff’s jurisdiction and powers run throughout the **entire county**, regardless of incorporated cities or other independent districts/governmental entities.
- Jurisdiction is concurrent with any city, district, or other law enforcement agency that has jurisdiction inside a city or district.
- A county’s board of county commissioners (or other county legislative body) may **not** maintain or establish a police department or other policing entity for the unincorporated areas.
- Only the duly elected sheriff may provide policing and police functions in the unincorporated areas (unless state law otherwise authorizes).
- Counties may not contract with or engage a city’s or district’s police department to provide sheriff services (including policing) in unincorporated areas.
- This does not affect state or federal agency jurisdiction or prohibit mutual aid agreements.

### Related Provisions
- **Deputies** (§ 30.07): Sheriffs may appoint deputies who have the same power as the sheriff; the sheriff remains responsible for their neglect or default.
- **Independence** (§ 30.53): Sheriffs retain independence regarding purchase/procurement of supplies and equipment, selection of personnel, and hiring/firing/setting salaries (subject to any applicable civil service systems).
- Other sections in Chapter 30 address bonds, succession of office, levying on writs/process, budgets, fees, liability insurance, and more.
- Additional duties appear in other chapters (e.g., court security funding coordination under Art. V of the Constitution and related statutes; traffic and other enforcement powers).

**In summary**, Florida sheriffs are constitutional officers whose core statutory powers emphasize execution of court process, court attendance and security, preservation of the peace (including warrantless arrests for disturbances and suppression of riots), exclusive law enforcement in unincorporated areas, concurrent jurisdiction countywide, and specific modern duties such as school guardian program support. Their authority is defined and limited by state constitution and statutes; they remain subject to state and federal law. 

